In <1992Mar23.082808.26266@demon.co.uk> printf@cix.compulink.co.uk writes:

> Possible minor spoiler on AC from enchant armour scrolls.
> 
> Someone posted that it was possible to get high armour enchantments because
> of the way scrolls of enchant armour work. You can enchant ordinary armour
> when it is less than +3 without any risk or an elvish mithril coat
> when less than +5. When you are over this enchantment each scroll has
> a 1/current enchantment chance of evaporating it. This means that if
> you enchant a elven mitril coat, starting at +5 you can get it to +43
> with a 50/50 chance of losing it(if all scrolls enchant by 3) or to
> about +17 if the scrolls enchant by 1,2 or 3 randomly, again with a 50/50

This is incorrect. You don't have a (1/current plus) probability of 
evaporation; you have a (1/current plus) probability of *not* evaporating.
Thus, starting with +3 armor (not mithril), your chance getting, say, +18
armor (assuming +3 per scroll) is 1/(6 * 9 * 12 * 15), or about .01%.
In other words, not bloody likely.

